A||||5||HEFTY13||Need a Fiero friendly mechanic||||8||
Z||000000||HEFTY13||01-25-2000||05:45 PM||||In the central Ohio area.I took my 87 GT to get the ign modual and pick up coil replaced, car ran great, for a day. Started missing real bad, I noticed they didn't put my plug wires back in the wire holders, they melted and were arching to my exhust manifold. I replaced them and now when maintaning a constant speed (35 mph)the car jerks around, not like it's missing, more like a hesatation or surge. I know I need to replace the AIC sensor, at idle it jumps around. I won't take the car back there, every mechanic I call asks why I got a Feiro, and I definatly won't take it to one of those types.||||reg||8
Z||000001||william_thorniii||01-25-2000||06:00 PM||||Hefty,<P>Look up David Rodabaugh.  He is the most intelligent Fierofile that I have ever met.  He lives in Columbus and actually does work for a small fee and parts.  If need be, I can find out his contact information for you.  <P>Or you could always check under one of the many Ohio or location related posts to find others in your area.  Chadman is up there, think that there are others as well.  They might be willing to lend some instruction.  <P>Or you could just learn the workings of your car.  The first step is to go to <A HREF="http://www.helm-inc.com" TARGET=_blank>www.helm-inc.com</A>  and purchase a factory repair manual.  That will lead you through almost any problem that should arise.  <P>Best of luck,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||4
Z||000002||ggarsk||01-25-2000||07:39 PM||||Your going to have to become your own friendly Fiero mechanic. I've found out that most shops won't touch a Fiero. Of course I've always worked on my own cars. The only difference is the Fiero is much funnier to drive when you get it back running! That makes up for the burnt arms,skinned knuckles and various cramps from bending at all angles!||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||01-26-2000||09:02 AM||||Hey Hefty, you might want to give my mechanic a call - Mark at S & S Auto.  He's on Riverside Drive in Dublin - 793-0410.  Fieros aren't his favorite car to work on, but he does good work at a reasonable price, and in 12 years, he has never once tried to f**k me on any job.  My one word of caution is that he works alone and he's pretty busy, so if you're taking your car in for something serious, plan to be without it for a week or so.  Mark has done all the serious work on every Fiero I've ever owned (including a clutch and lower ball joints on my 88 Coupe).<P>If you do take your car to Mark, please do me a favor and tell him I referred you (Chad Mankins).||||reg||1
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||01-26-2000||12:57 PM||||I would have to agree whole heartedly with ggarsk, long ago when I got my Fiero I had the unpleasant experience, (I think its a rite of pasage for a Fiero owner) of being ridiculed for my car. Get to know your car, the engine, know the sounds that it makes and when. There will be people that tell you out right, "its supposed to do that." I would then reply why is it doing it now?      <BR>     Even if you get a great mechanic, you can be of help to him if you can tell him the exact symptoms of what happened instead of giving it to him on a hook and saying, it broke. It helps them to have an idea of where to start looking ESPECIALLY if its an intermittant problem.<P>Good luck to you! And of course we are here to help as much as we can!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||2
Z||000005||HEFTY13||01-26-2000||01:42 PM||||Thanks for the advice and contacts. I usually try to tackle any mechanical stuff myself, usually on older carb'd rides. The Fiero does intimate me a little with all of the electrical and senor componets, that and a lack of a garage to work in, it's cold outside !||||reg||1
